# pg_recall: Cognitive Memory Primitives for Postgres

A Postgres extension that implements neuroscience-inspired memory primitives —
ACT-R activation, Hebbian association learning, Bayesian confidence, and
Ebbinghaus decay — as composable SQL functions over pgvector-enabled tables.

Memories decay with time, strengthen through use, form associations
automatically through co-activation, and track confidence via Bayesian
updating. The primary interface is a single recall() function that fuses
vector similarity, full-text search, temporal relevance, and associative
context into one ranked result set.

---

CONSTRAINT: rust_extension
  This extension is built in Rust using pgrx. Not optional. Agents must not
  choose a different language, PL/pgSQL implementation, or foreign data wrapper
  approach. The extension compiles to a shared library loaded by Postgres.
  All SQL-exposed functions are registered via pgrx #[pg_extern] macros.

CONSTRAINT: version_requirements
  | Dependency | Version    | Notes                              |
  |------------|------------|------------------------------------|
  | Rust       | 1.94+      | Stable toolchain                   |
  | pgrx       | 0.17.x     | With pg18 feature flag              |
  | PostgreSQL | 18.3+      | Target runtime                     |
  | pgvector   | 0.8.2+     | Extension control: requires='vector'|

CONSTRAINT: extension_identity
  Extension is named pg_recall. All objects live in the pg_recall schema.
  Extension version is 0.1.0. The .control file declares
  requires = 'vector' so pgvector must be installed first.

CONSTRAINT: multi_tenancy
  All memory-scoped tables include a workspace_id UUID column. The extension
  does not enforce RLS itself but is designed to work with Postgres RLS
  policies on workspace_id. Every query-path function accepts workspace_id
  and filters on it.

CONSTRAINT: v01_scope
  v0.1 does not include an autonomous background worker, shared_preload_libraries
  requirement, adaptive polling, or live worker stats. Hebbian processing is
  driven explicitly through SQL-callable batch functions. The background worker
  is deferred to v0.2.

---

DATA: recall_result
  mneme_id:      uuid
  score:         float, final composite score
  content_match: float, vector + FTS fusion component
  activation:    float, ACT-R base-level activation
  hebbian_boost: float, association weight sum from co-candidates
  confidence:    float, current Bayesian confidence
  concept:       text
  content:       text

DATA: score_weights
  semantic:      float, default 0.6, weight for vector cosine similarity
  fts:           float, default 0.4, weight for full-text search rank
  actr_decay:    float, default 0.5, ACT-R power-law decay exponent
  hebbian_scale: float, default 4.0, multiplier for Hebbian boost in activation

---

FUNCTION: bootstrap_extension_skeleton() -> compiling pgrx project

  The foundation that all other work builds on. Produces a pgrx project
  that compiles, boots Postgres, and accepts CREATE EXTENSION pg_recall.

  RULES:
    - Extension named pg_recall in the pg_recall schema
    - pgrx project configured with pg18 feature flag and pgvector dependency
    - All source modules exist as stubs with placeholder public signatures
    - recall_result and score_weights composite types have stub definitions
    - All scoring, recall, and hebbian function signatures are stubbed
    - Project compiles and boots with cargo pgrx run
    - CREATE EXTENSION pg_recall succeeds on the booted instance

  DONE_WHEN:
    - cargo pgrx run boots Postgres with the extension loadable
    - CREATE EXTENSION pg_recall creates the pg_recall schema without errors
    - All downstream module files exist in the source tree as compilable stubs

  EXAMPLES:
    greenfield workspace
      -> compiling pgrx project, cargo pgrx run boots cleanly
    CREATE EXTENSION pg_recall on booted instance
      -> pg_recall schema exists, no errors

  ERRORS:
    - cargo pgrx run fails -> resolve compilation errors before any downstream work
    - CREATE EXTENSION fails -> extension control file or SQL wiring is malformed

  WRITES:
    - project skeleton: Cargo.toml, control file, source modules, test stubs

---

FUNCTION: define_composite_types() -> SQL-visible composite types

  Define the two composite types used by recall and scoring functions.

  RULES:
    - recall_result type registered in pg_recall schema with all fields from DATA
    - score_weights type registered in pg_recall schema with all fields from DATA
    - Both types are usable in SQL: castable, selectable, returnable from functions
    - Types are importable by other Rust modules in the same crate

  DONE_WHEN:
    - Casting a valid literal to pg_recall.recall_result succeeds in SQL
    - Casting a valid literal to pg_recall.score_weights succeeds in SQL
    - Downstream Rust modules can reference the type definitions

  EXAMPLES:
    SELECT (gen_random_uuid(), 0.9, 0.8, 2.1, 0.3, 0.7, 'k8s', 'pod scheduling')::pg_recall.recall_result
      -> valid composite value, all fields accessible
    SELECT (0.7, 0.3, 0.5, 4.0)::pg_recall.score_weights
      -> valid composite value with custom weights
    SELECT r.score, r.concept FROM unnest(ARRAY[...]) AS r(pg_recall.recall_result)
      -> fields individually selectable from composite

  ERRORS:
    - Type not found in pg_recall schema -> pgrx registration is incorrect
    - Field count mismatch on cast -> type definition does not match DATA spec

  READS:
    - project skeleton from bootstrap

  WRITES:
    - types module with recall_result and score_weights definitions

---

FUNCTION: implement_scoring_primitives() -> four SQL-callable scoring functions

  Implement the pure cognitive scoring functions. These are stateless
  computations with no side effects, callable independently by users who
  want to compose custom retrieval pipelines.

  RULES:
    - softplus(x float) returns ln(1 + exp(x))
    - softplus returns x directly when x > 20 to prevent exp overflow
    - actr_activation(access_count int, last_access timestamptz) returns
      base-level activation: B = ln(n+1) - d * ln(max(age_days, 1/1440) / (n+1))
      where n = access_count + 1, d = 0.5, age_days = days since last_access,
      and 1/1440 is the one-minute age floor
    - ebbinghaus_decay(last_access timestamptz, access_count int, created_at timestamptz)
      returns retention factor with spacing-aware stability:
      stability = clamp(ln(n+1)*20 * (1 + 0.5*tanh(avg_spacing/7)), 14, 365),
      retention = max(0.05, exp(-days_since / stability)),
      where avg_spacing = lifespan_days / max(access_count, 1)
    - bayesian_update(prior float, evidence float) returns posterior with
      Laplace smoothing: result = 0.95 * (prior*ev / max(prior*ev + (1-prior)*(1-ev), 1e-9)) + 0.025
    - bayesian_update result is always in [0.025, 0.975], never reaching 0 or 1
    - All four functions are immutable and parallel_safe in the pg_recall schema

  DONE_WHEN:
    - All four functions callable from SQL in the pg_recall schema
    - Reference values match within reasonable floating-point tolerance
    - Functions marked immutable and parallel_safe

  EXAMPLES:
    softplus(0.0)   -> ~0.6931 (ln(2), normal case)
    softplus(2.0)   -> ~2.1269 (normal case)
    softplus(-5.0)  -> ~0.0067 (negative input, near-zero output)
    softplus(25.0)  -> 25.0    (overflow guard, x > 20)

    actr_activation(13, now() - interval '10 days')
      -> ~2.08 (frequently accessed, moderate age: high activation)
    actr_activation(0, now() - interval '1400 days')
      -> ~-3.27 (never re-accessed, very old: low activation)

    ebbinghaus_decay(now()-'30 days', 50, now()-'180 days')
      -> ~0.78 (50 accesses spaced over 6 months: high stability, slow decay)
    ebbinghaus_decay(now()-'30 days', 50, now()-'1 day')
      -> ~0.12 (50 accesses crammed in 1 day: low stability, fast decay)

    bayesian_update(0.5, 0.95)  -> ~0.925 (neutral prior + strong confirmation)
    bayesian_update(0.8, 0.10)  -> ~0.32  (confident prior + contradiction)
    bayesian_update(0.32, 0.10) -> ~0.078 (chained contradiction, confidence collapses)

  ERRORS:
    - any unhandled condition -> fail with descriptive message

  READS:
    - project skeleton from bootstrap

  WRITES:
    - scoring module with all four function implementations

---

FUNCTION: create_extension_schema() -> tables, indexes, constraints

  Define the SQL install script that CREATE EXTENSION pg_recall executes.
  Produces the three core tables, all indexes, and all constraints.

  RULES:
    - mnemes table: id (uuid PK), workspace_id (uuid NOT NULL),
      concept (text NOT NULL), content (text NOT NULL),
      embedding (vector(384) NOT NULL), search_vector (generated tsvector from
      setweight(to_tsvector('english', concept), 'A') ||
      setweight(to_tsvector('english', content), 'B')),
      confidence (float default 0.5), access_count (int default 0),
      last_access (timestamptz default now()), created_at (timestamptz default now()),
      state (text default 'active', CHECK in active/archived/dormant)
    - associations table: src_id and dst_id (uuid FK to mnemes, composite PK),
      weight (float default 0.01), co_activations (int default 0),
      updated_at (timestamptz default now()), CHECK (src_id < dst_id)
    - co_activation_queue table: id (bigserial PK), workspace_id (uuid NOT NULL),
      mneme_ids (uuid[] NOT NULL), scores (float[] NOT NULL),
      created_at (timestamptz default now())
    - HNSW index on mnemes(embedding) with vector_cosine_ops
    - GIN index on mnemes(search_vector)
    - B-tree index on mnemes(workspace_id, last_access DESC)
    - B-tree index on associations(dst_id, src_id)
    - Embedding dimension hardcoded to 384 for v0.1

  DONE_WHEN:
    - All three tables exist in pg_recall schema after CREATE EXTENSION
    - CHECK constraint on associations rejects src_id >= dst_id
    - CHECK constraint on mnemes.state rejects values outside active/archived/dormant
    - search_vector auto-populates when concept or content is inserted or updated
    - All four indexes are present

  EXAMPLES:
    CREATE EXTENSION pg_recall (pgvector already installed)
      -> all three tables visible in pg_recall schema
    INSERT INTO pg_recall.mnemes with concept='k8s' and content='pod scheduling'
      -> search_vector column auto-populated with weighted tsvector
    INSERT INTO pg_recall.associations with src_id >= dst_id
      -> CHECK violation error, rejected
    INSERT INTO pg_recall.mnemes with state='invalid'
      -> CHECK violation error, rejected
    INSERT INTO pg_recall.mnemes with state='dormant'
      -> succeeds, valid state value

  ERRORS:
    - pgvector extension not installed -> CREATE EXTENSION fails with dependency error
    - any unhandled condition -> fail with descriptive message

  READS:
    - project skeleton from bootstrap

  WRITES:
    - SQL install script (pg_recall--0.1.0.sql)

---

FUNCTION: implement_hebbian_helpers() -> six SQL-callable Hebbian and confidence functions

  Implement the functions that drive Hebbian learning and confidence tracking.
  These allow operators to process co-activation events explicitly from SQL,
  pg_cron, or external schedulers without requiring an autonomous background worker.

  RULES:
    - record_co_activation(workspace_id uuid, mneme_ids uuid[], scores float[])
      inserts one row into co_activation_queue
    - record_co_activation rejects calls where mneme_ids and scores arrays
      have different lengths
    - get_associations(mneme_id uuid, min_weight float DEFAULT 0.01) returns
      all associations for the mneme from both directions (where mneme is src
      or dst), with the other ID as related_id and the weight, ordered by
      weight descending
    - get_associations returns empty result set when no associations exist
      above the weight threshold
    - update_confidence(mneme_id uuid, evidence float) atomically reads the
      mneme's current confidence, applies bayesian_update, writes back, and
      returns the new confidence
    - confirm_recall(mneme_ids uuid[]) applies evidence=0.95 to every
      provided mneme ID via update_confidence
    - process_co_activation_batch(batch_limit int DEFAULT 100) consumes up to
      batch_limit rows from co_activation_queue: generates all canonical (i,j)
      pairs where i < j from each event's mneme_ids, aggregates pair signals
      as sum(score_i * score_j), upserts associations with log-space weight
      update (new = min(1.0, exp(ln(current) + signal * ln(1.01)))), seeds
      cold-start associations at 0.01, updates mnemes.access_count and
      mnemes.last_access for all referenced mnemes, deletes consumed queue
      rows, and returns the number of rows processed
    - process_co_activation_batch returns 0 when the queue is empty
    - process_all_pending_co_activations() repeatedly calls batch processing
      until the queue is drained and returns total rows processed

  DONE_WHEN:
    - All six functions callable from SQL in the pg_recall schema
    - Co-activation events flow from queue to association weight updates
    - Access tracking (access_count, last_access) updates on batch processing
    - Queue rows are deleted after successful processing

  EXAMPLES:
    record_co_activation('ws1', ARRAY['a','b','c']::uuid[], ARRAY[0.9,0.7,0.5])
      -> one row inserted into co_activation_queue
    record_co_activation('ws1', ARRAY['a','b']::uuid[], ARRAY[0.9]::float[])
      -> error: array length mismatch

    get_associations('mneme-1', 0.01) where mneme-1 has associations as both src and dst
      -> unified result set from both directions, ordered by weight desc
    get_associations('mneme-1', 0.01) where mneme-1 has no associations
      -> empty result set
    get_associations('mneme-1', 0.5) where all associations have weight < 0.5
      -> empty result set (filtered by min_weight)

    update_confidence('mneme-1', 0.95) where current confidence is 0.5
      -> returns ~0.925, mneme's confidence column now ~0.925
    update_confidence('mneme-1', 0.10) where current confidence is 0.8
      -> returns ~0.32, confidence decreased by contradiction

    confirm_recall(ARRAY['m1','m2','m3']::uuid[])
      -> all three mnemes have confidence updated with evidence=0.95

    process_co_activation_batch(100) with 3 pending queue rows
      -> returns 3, associations upserted for all pairs, access_count incremented,
         last_access updated, consumed queue rows deleted
    process_co_activation_batch(100) with empty queue
      -> returns 0, no side effects
    process_co_activation_batch(100) with co-activation containing new pair
      -> new association row created with weight seeded at 0.01 then reinforced

    process_all_pending_co_activations() with 250 pending rows
      -> returns 250, queue fully drained across multiple batch cycles

  ERRORS:
    - mneme_ids and scores arrays have different lengths -> fail with descriptive message
    - mneme_id not found in mnemes table -> fail with descriptive message
    - batch transaction fails -> queue rows remain for retry on next call
    - any unhandled condition -> fail with descriptive message

  READS:
    - scoring module (uses bayesian_update for confidence operations)
    - schema (operates on mnemes, associations, co_activation_queue tables)

  WRITES:
    - hebbian module with all six function implementations

---

FUNCTION: implement_composite_recall(workspace_id uuid, query_text text, query_embedding vector(384), limit_n int, min_confidence float, weights score_weights) -> SETOF recall_result

  The primary retrieval entry point. Fuses vector similarity, full-text search,
  ACT-R temporal activation, Hebbian association strength, and Bayesian
  confidence into a single ranked result set.

  RULES:
    - Candidate pool is the union of HNSW nearest neighbors and FTS ts_rank
      matches, deduplicated, with pool size = 3 * limit_n
    - content_match = weights.semantic * vector_cosine_similarity +
      weights.fts * tanh(fts_rank)
    - Hebbian boost for each candidate is the sum of association weights
      to any other candidate in the current result set
    - temporal_weight = softplus(actr_activation + weights.hebbian_scale *
      hebbian_boost) / (1 + softplus(0))
    - Composite score = content_match * temporal_weight * confidence
    - Only mnemes with state='active' and confidence >= min_confidence
      are included in results
    - Results filtered by workspace_id
    - Results ordered by composite score descending, limited to limit_n
    - After ranking, the top-n mneme IDs and their scores are enqueued
      as a co-activation event via record_co_activation
    - recall does NOT update access_count or last_access directly
    - When weights parameter is NULL, defaults are used:
      semantic=0.6, fts=0.4, actr_decay=0.5, hebbian_scale=4.0

  DONE_WHEN:
    - recall() callable from SQL, returns SETOF pg_recall.recall_result
    - Ranking reflects all five scoring signals
    - Co-activation event enqueued after each recall
    - Workspace and confidence filtering behave correctly
    - Default weights applied when weights parameter is NULL

  EXAMPLES:
    recall('ws1', 'kubernetes pod scheduling', embedding, 10, 0.0, NULL)
      -> up to 10 results ranked by composite score, co-activation event enqueued,
         default weights used

    recall('ws1', 'database migration', embedding, 5, 0.7, NULL)
      -> only mnemes with confidence >= 0.7 included, others excluded even if
         semantically similar

    recall('ws1', 'query', embedding, 10, 0.0, (0.8, 0.2, 0.5, 2.0)::pg_recall.score_weights)
      -> custom weights applied: heavier semantic, lighter FTS, reduced hebbian scale

    recall('ws1', 'nonexistent topic', embedding, 10, 0.0, NULL)
      where no mnemes match at all
      -> empty result set, co-activation event still enqueued (with empty arrays)

    recall('ws1', 'topic', embedding, 10, 0.0, NULL)
      where two returned mnemes have a strong association
      -> those mnemes receive hebbian_boost > 0, potentially reranked higher
         than they would be on content_match alone

    recall('ws1', 'topic', embedding, 10, 0.0, NULL)
      -> co_activation_queue contains a new row with the returned mneme IDs and scores

  ERRORS:
    - workspace_id is NULL -> fail with descriptive message
    - query_embedding has wrong dimensions -> fail with descriptive message
    - any unhandled condition -> fail with descriptive message

  READS:
    - types module (recall_result, score_weights)
    - scoring module (actr_activation, softplus)
    - schema (mnemes, associations tables)
    - hebbian module (record_co_activation, get_associations)

  WRITES:
    - recall module with composite recall function implementation

---

FUNCTION: integrate_and_package() -> releasable extension

  Final integration pass. Wire all independently built modules into a
  coherent, installable extension and verify end-to-end behavior.

  RULES:
    - CREATE EXTENSION vector followed by CREATE EXTENSION pg_recall
      succeeds on a clean database
    - All SQL-visible functions, types, and tables are accessible after install
    - End-to-end flow works: insert mnemes with embeddings, call recall(),
      observe co-activation events queued, process them with
      process_co_activation_batch(), observe associations formed, call recall()
      again and observe hebbian_boost values in results
    - Extension version reported as 0.1.0
    - No orphaned or conflicting symbol registrations between modules

  DONE_WHEN:
    - Clean install on fresh Postgres 18.3+ with pgvector 0.8.2+ succeeds
    - Full recall-learn-recall cycle produces observable association changes
    - All functions, types, and tables documented in README with usage examples
    - No compilation warnings from pgrx

  EXAMPLES:
    Fresh database: CREATE EXTENSION vector; CREATE EXTENSION pg_recall;
      -> all pg_recall schema objects visible, no errors

    Insert 3 mnemes, call recall() with a related query
      -> ranked results returned with scores, content_match, activation,
         confidence fields populated

    After recall(), call process_co_activation_batch()
      -> associations created between co-activated mnemes,
         get_associations() returns the new links

    Second recall() after associations formed
      -> hebbian_boost field is non-zero for associated mnemes,
         demonstrating the learn-from-usage feedback loop

  ERRORS:
    - pgvector not installed -> CREATE EXTENSION pg_recall fails with clear dependency message
    - Rust function signature does not match SQL expectation -> compilation error
    - any unhandled condition -> fail with descriptive message

  READS:
    - all modules: types, scoring, schema, hebbian, recall

  WRITES:
    - final lib.rs wiring
    - README with installation and usage documentation
    - end-to-end integration tests
